---
import AdeGuide from '../../../components/ade/AdeGuide.astro';
import { law, reciprocityCountries, newResident, fees, itd } from '../../../data/ade-eligibility';

const recip = reciprocityCountries;

const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'Can I trade my foreign driver license for a Texas one?',
    a: `Only if it was issued by a U.S. territory, Canada, or one of the five countries Texas holds reciprocity with: ${recip.join(', ')}. Every other foreign license has to go through the full Texas process: application, vision test, knowledge test and driving skills test, plus the six-hour adult driver education course if you are 18 through 24.`,
  },
  {
    q: 'I have driven for ten years in my home country. Do I really need a beginner course?',
    a: 'If you are 18 through 24 and your license was not issued by a U.S. state, a U.S. territory, Canada or a reciprocity country, then yes. Texas Transportation Code §521.1601 ties the requirement to age and to holding a license issued by another state, not to how long you have been driving. It is a blunt rule and it catches experienced drivers.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How long can I drive in Texas on my foreign license?',
    a: `DPS says you can legally drive on a valid, unexpired license from another U.S. state, U.S. territory, Canadian province or qualifying country for up to ${newResident.graceDays} days after moving to Texas. After that you need a Texas license.`,
  },
  {
    q: 'Does my license need to be translated?',
    a: `Yes, if it is not in English or Spanish. DPS requires the translation to be done by a translation service or your consulate before you arrive in Texas, so this is something to arrange before you travel rather than after.`,
  },
  {
    q: 'I am on a student or work visa. Can I get a Texas license?',
    a: `Yes. Texas issues a limited term driver license to temporary visitors who can show lawful presence. It costs $${fees.limitedTermVisitor} and expires when your period of lawful presence expires, or after one year if your status is recorded as "duration of status". The driver education rule works the same way: required if you are 18 through 24 and this is your first U.S.-state license.`,
  },
  {
    q: 'Am I exempt at 25 or older?',
    a: 'From the course, yes. DPS states there are no driver education requirements at 25 and older. You would still take the knowledge and skills exams, since a non-reciprocity foreign license does not waive them, and you would still need an Impact Texas Adult Drivers certificate before the skills test.',
  },
];
---

<AdeGuide
  title="Moving to Texas With a Foreign Driver License: What You Actually Need | Road Ready Safety"
  description="A license from another country usually does not exempt you from Texas adult driver education. Texas has reciprocity with only five countries. Here is the rule and what it means."
  url="/adult-drivers-ed/texas/foreign-license-texas"
  eyebrow="New residents"
  h1="Moving to Texas with a license from another country"
  lede="This is the case Texas rules handle least intuitively. An out-of-state license exempts you from adult driver education. A license from most other countries does not, no matter how long you have been driving on it."
  faqs={faqs}
  sources={[
    { label: 'Tex. Transp. Code §521.1601, Driver Education Required; Exception', href: 'https://statutes.capitol.texas.gov/Docs/TN/htm/TN.521.htm' },
    { label: 'Texas DPS, Moving to Texas: A Guide to Driver Licenses and IDs', href: 'https://www.dps.texas.gov/section/driver-license/moving-texas' },
    { label: 'Texas DPS, Apply for a Texas Driver License', href: 'https://www.dps.texas.gov/section/driver-license/apply-texas-driver-license' },
    { label: 'Texas DPS, Driver License Fees', href: 'https://www.dps.texas.gov/section/driver-license/driver-license-fees' },
  ]}
>

  <section class="flex flex-col gap-4">
    <h2 class="text-[22px] font-bold text-text leading-8" style="font-family:'Outfit',sans-serif;">The sentence that decides it</h2>
    <p class="text-[17px] text-text-body leading-[30px]">
      Texas Transportation Code §521.1601 requires driver education before the state will license
      anyone under {law.ageCeiling}. Subsection (b) is the entire exemption, and it is one line:
    </p>
    <blockquote class="border-l-4 border-border pl-5 py-1 m-0">
      <p class="text-[17px] text-text-body leading-[30px] italic m-0">&ldquo;{law.exemptionText}&rdquo;</p>
      <cite class="text-sm text-text-muted not-italic">&mdash; Tex. Transp. Code §521.1601(b)</cite>
    </blockquote>
    <p class="text-[17px] text-text-body leading-[30px]">
      <strong>Another state.</strong> Not another country. A license from Louisiana exempts a
      23-year-old; a license from Honduras does not. That is not an oversight anyone has read into
      the statute &mdash; DPS applies it the same way, and the practical consequence is that a lot of
      experienced adult drivers arrive in Texas and discover they are treated as beginners.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="flex flex-col gap-4">
    <h2 class="text-[22px] font-bold text-text leading-8" style="font-family:'Outfit',sans-serif;">The exceptions DPS does make</h2>
    <p class="text-[17px] text-text-body leading-[30px]">
      DPS is slightly more generous than the bare statute. From its Moving to Texas page:
    </p>
    <ul class="flex flex-col gap-3 pl-5 m-0 list-disc marker:text-text-subtle">
      <li class="text-[17px] text-text-body leading-[30px]">
        <strong>A U.S. state, a U.S. territory, or Canada.</strong> Surrender the license and you are
        not required to take the knowledge or skills exams.
      </li>
      <li class="text-[17px] text-text-body leading-[30px]">
        <strong>Reciprocity countries &mdash; {recip.slice(0, -1).join(', ')} and {recip.at(-1)}.</strong>{' '}
        A valid, unexpired license from one of these five may waive the knowledge and skills exams,
        unless you choose to keep your out-of-country license.
      </li>
      <li class="text-[17px] text-text-body leading-[30px]">
        <strong>Everywhere else.</strong> Full process, and the six-hour adult driver education
        course if you are {law.requiredBand}.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p class="text-[17px] text-text-body leading-[30px]">
      Five countries is a short list. Mexico is not on it. Neither are India, the Philippines,
      Vietnam, China, Nigeria, Brazil, El Salvador, Guatemala or Honduras &mdash; between them, most
      of the people who move to Texas with a license already in their pocket.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="flex flex-col gap-4">
    <h2 class="text-[22px] font-bold text-text leading-8" style="font-family:'Outfit',sans-serif;">Three things to do before you arrive</h2>
    <ul class="flex flex-col gap-3 pl-5 m-0 list-disc marker:text-text-subtle">
      <li class="text-[17px] text-text-body leading-[30px]">
        <strong>Get the license translated.</strong> DPS: &ldquo;{newResident.translationRule}&rdquo;
        A translation service or your consulate can do it; the counter will not.
      </li>
      <li class="text-[17px] text-text-body leading-[30px]">
        <strong>Count your {newResident.graceDays} days.</strong> You may drive on a valid, unexpired
        license from another U.S. state, U.S. territory, Canadian province or qualifying country for
        up to {newResident.graceDays} days after moving. That is the window to get everything else done.
      </li>
      <li class="text-[17px] text-text-body leading-[30px]">
        <strong>Book the appointment early.</strong> DPS driver license offices are appointment only,
        and the driving skills test is the slowest thing to get. See{' '}
        <a class="underline hover:no-underline" href="/adult-drivers-ed/texas/impact-texas-adult-drivers">how the queue interacts with the {itd.certValidDays}-day ITD certificate</a>.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="flex flex-col gap-4">
    <h2 class="text-[22px] font-bold text-text leading-8" style="font-family:'Outfit',sans-serif;">If you are here on a visa</h2>
    <p class="text-[17px] text-text-body leading-[30px]">
      Texas issues a limited term driver license to temporary visitors who can document lawful
      presence. The fee is ${fees.limitedTermVisitor}, and it expires when your period of lawful
      presence expires &mdash; or after one year, if your status is recorded as &ldquo;duration of
      status&rdquo;. The driver education question is unchanged: it depends on your age and on who
      issued any license you already hold.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="flex flex-col gap-4">
    <h2 class="text-[22px] font-bold text-text leading-8" style="font-family:'Outfit',sans-serif;">Read next</h2>
    <ul class="flex flex-col gap-3 pl-5 m-0 list-disc marker:text-text-subtle">
      <li class="text-[17px] text-text-body leading-[30px]"><a class="underline hover:no-underline" href="/adult-drivers-ed/texas/do-i-need-adult-drivers-ed">Do you need adult drivers ed in Texas? The rule by age and license</a></li>
      <li class="text-[17px] text-text-body leading-[30px]"><a class="underline hover:no-underline" href="/adult-drivers-ed/texas/how-to-get-a-texas-license-as-an-adult">How to get a Texas driver license as an adult</a></li>
      <li class="text-[17px] text-text-body leading-[30px]"><a class="underline hover:no-underline" href="/adult-drivers-ed/texas/new-resident-out-of-state-license">Moving to Texas with an out-of-state license</a></li>
    </ul>
  </section>

</AdeGuide>
